{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["And He said, <b>and He shall deliver their kings into your hand, and you shall destroy their name</b> — until they have no name abroad, and they are to destroy all who stand before them. This is like the matter that appears in Joshua (Joshua 11:20), for it was from HaShem to strengthen their hearts to meet the battle against Israel in order to devote them to destruction, and no king was left to them in the land, for all their kings had fallen. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 7:22), lest the beasts of the field multiply against you — but is it not so that if they do His will, they need not fear the beasts, as it is said (Job 5:23), and the beast of the field shall be at peace with you? Rather, it was revealed before Him that they were destined to sin. And these are the words of our Sages (Sifrei). And even though this passage was spoken as a promise, I have already mentioned (Ramban on Deuteronomy 4:25) that Moses our Master, in the repetition of the Torah, promises and warns and hints that their merit will not be sufficient to destroy them all at once and that He will cause an evil beast to cease from the land, as is promised to them in the section of If in My statutes (Leviticus 26:6), but rather it will be as He said: they will destroy the majority of them and their kings at the beginning, and afterward the remainder will perish."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The translation preserves Ramban's argumentative structure: the verse reading, the Joshua parallel, Rashi's citation with objection and resolution attributed to the Sages, and Ramban's own resolution referencing his earlier commentary. The phrase 'השבית חיה רעה' is rendered hyper-literally as 'cause an evil beast to cease'; the idiom means removal of harmful beasts but is kept literal per instructions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:46:44.444426493Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}